CloudBurrow patch 62 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1525). Applies after patch 0056; needs patches
0036 (change tables) and 0002.

A DROP TABLE cost the square of the number of tables the engine held.
Catalog.existsTable, which every table registration asks first, listed
the SimpleCatalog's every table name through the wasm bridge
(TableNames), and the root catalog holds each table under several name
paths and each dataset's INFORMATION_SCHEMA views; a DROP rebuilds the
whole catalog (resetCatalog), registering every table again, and a DROP
TABLE rebuilt it twice, once for the table and once for its change
table (0036). Measured natively on an Apple silicon Mac: a DROP TABLE
took 18 ms with no other table, 0.67 s with 200 and 2.5 s with 400, and
a CREATE TABLE 2 ms and 25 ms. Through CloudBurrow's front, which keeps
a deleted table's history in a table of its own and makes and drops a
scratch table for a load into a partition, an instance that had run the
BigQuery compat suite a while took tens of seconds to delete a dataset
and to run a load.

The catalog now keeps the names each SimpleCatalog holds (tableNames),
listed once and kept as tables are added (a SimpleCatalog never removes
one; resetCatalog starts over with its new catalogs), and the
SimpleTable built for each spec and each INFORMATION_SCHEMA view
(tableHandles, infoSchemaHandles), which a rebuild adds again rather
than building anew; and a DROP TABLE deletes the table's spec and its
change table's in one rebuild (DeleteTableSpecs). The same DROP TABLE
takes 9 ms with no other table and 26 ms with 400, and a CREATE TABLE
2 ms and 4 ms. What the catalog resolves is unchanged.
catalog_rebuild_test.go fails without the patch (a DROP with 300 tables
took 1.3 s) and passes with it.

